WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.763
Welcome.

2
00:00:00.763 --> 00:00:04.054
This briefing comes from
People: Training and Education,

3
00:00:04.054 --> 00:00:07.275
one of the books of the
Federal Modernization Workbook.

4
00:00:07.275 --> 00:00:09.696
It's written for CIOs and CISOs,

5
00:00:09.696 --> 00:00:14.150
workforce and training leads,
ISSOs, and help desk leads.

6
00:00:14.150 --> 00:00:15.563
I'll take four questions.

7
00:00:15.563 --> 00:00:18.612
Why do people, not tools, close the gap?

8
00:00:18.612 --> 00:00:20.213
What does the program teach?

9
00:00:20.213 --> 00:00:22.000
Who owns workforce readiness?

10
00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:25.212
And how do you run the curriculum
and measure whether it worked?

11
00:00:25.212 --> 00:00:29.312
The short version: every book before
this one changes what someone does.

12
00:00:29.312 --> 00:00:32.971
Tools enforce the new rules,
but people run the estate,

13
00:00:32.971 --> 00:00:35.799
and a program that trains last pays twice.

14
00:00:35.799 --> 00:00:38.150
Let's start with what tools can't do.

15
00:00:41.900 --> 00:00:43.800
Here's the gap in one example.

16
00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:48.938
A Conditional Access policy can require
phishing-resistant sign-in for every user.

17
00:00:48.938 --> 00:00:52.410
What it can't do is decide what
happens when a caller tells the

18
00:00:52.410 --> 00:00:55.951
help desk their security key is
lost and they need in right now.

19
00:00:55.951 --> 00:00:58.971
An access review can list
everyone with access,

20
00:00:58.971 --> 00:01:01.450
but it can't make the owner read the list.

21
00:01:01.450 --> 00:01:04.007
The program's view is that this technology

22
00:01:04.007 --> 00:01:06.638
moves risk from configuration to judgment.

23
00:01:06.638 --> 00:01:10.788
There are fewer human decisions left,
but each one carries more weight.

24
00:01:10.788 --> 00:01:14.665
And almost every book splits
a job into deciding and doing,

25
00:01:14.665 --> 00:01:20.238
and hands some decisions to people outside
IT, like HR staff and application owners.

26
00:01:20.238 --> 00:01:23.730
Someone who doesn't know the new
rule falls back on the old habit,

27
00:01:23.730 --> 00:01:27.088
which is exactly what the
modernization was meant to remove.

28
00:01:27.088 --> 00:01:29.050
So why not train at the end?

29
00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:37.300
Many programs train at the end:
build, migrate, then train.

30
00:01:37.300 --> 00:01:40.375
The program's point is that
this gets paid for twice.

31
00:01:40.375 --> 00:01:42.400
The first payment comes at cutover.

32
00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:45.700
Users who weren't taught the
new sign-in call the help desk.

33
00:01:45.700 --> 00:01:49.925
A help desk that wasn't taught the new
verification either turns away real

34
00:01:49.925 --> 00:01:54.288
callers or resets accounts without the
checks meant to stop impersonation.

35
00:01:54.288 --> 00:01:58.018
Administrators ask for standing
roles until things settle,

36
00:01:58.018 --> 00:02:01.226
and exceptions granted
under pressure tend to stay.

37
00:02:01.226 --> 00:02:04.722
The second payment comes afterward,
finding and removing the

38
00:02:04.722 --> 00:02:08.700
workarounds and unlearning the
habits, which is slower than learning.

39
00:02:08.700 --> 00:02:12.071
And federal rules already put
training before the change:

40
00:02:12.071 --> 00:02:16.075
before access, and whenever the
environment changes significantly.

41
00:02:16.075 --> 00:02:20.675
So training last also puts the agency
behind requirements it already has.

42
00:02:20.675 --> 00:02:22.800
Let's look at those requirements.

43
00:02:26.550 --> 00:02:30.175
FISMA is a staffing requirement
as much as a course requirement.

44
00:02:30.175 --> 00:02:33.781
The agency head must have enough
trained people to comply,

45
00:02:33.781 --> 00:02:38.288
and the CIO trains and oversees those
with significant security duties.

46
00:02:38.288 --> 00:02:43.140
OPM's rule names five audiences, from
every user to IT operations staff,

47
00:02:43.140 --> 00:02:47.825
and agencies must code their cyber
positions to the NICE Framework,

48
00:02:47.825 --> 00:02:50.000
NIST's vocabulary of work roles.

49
00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:52.163
But those codes haven't been reliable.

50
00:02:52.163 --> 00:02:57.630
GAO found most agencies had miscoded a
share of their IT management positions,

51
00:02:57.630 --> 00:03:02.088
so the program treats codes as one
roster input, not settled fact.

52
00:03:02.088 --> 00:03:04.962
Just as important is what
the rules don't require.

53
00:03:04.962 --> 00:03:08.361
There's no civilian certification
mandate, and the Defense

54
00:03:08.361 --> 00:03:12.524
Department's certification program
doesn't apply to civilian agencies.

55
00:03:12.524 --> 00:03:15.350
Now, what the program actually teaches.

56
00:03:19.100 --> 00:03:21.625
The program separates
education from training.

57
00:03:21.625 --> 00:03:27.125
Education builds the mental model, for
everyone, before anyone touches a tool.

58
00:03:27.125 --> 00:03:31.675
It teaches six ideas in order, because
each depends on the one before.

59
00:03:31.675 --> 00:03:34.825
For example: identity
is the starting point,

60
00:03:34.825 --> 00:03:39.725
one map replaces many inventories,
and drift is the feedback signal.

61
00:03:39.725 --> 00:03:44.197
Someone who holds those ideas understands
why the help desk now asks for

62
00:03:44.197 --> 00:03:47.476
verification, why a role is
activated instead of held,

63
00:03:47.476 --> 00:03:49.637
and why a screenshot isn't evidence.

64
00:03:49.637 --> 00:03:53.314
Orientation comes in a few
forms, including a walkthrough,

65
00:03:53.314 --> 00:03:57.987
a self-serve written path, and a short
briefing for auditors and assessors.

66
00:03:57.987 --> 00:04:01.824
Then training follows, by role,
in four tracks: compliance,

67
00:04:01.824 --> 00:04:06.640
for people who evidence controls;
implementation, for people who build;

68
00:04:06.640 --> 00:04:11.538
vendor training and labs; and end-user
training for everyone who signs in.

69
00:04:11.538 --> 00:04:14.090
Let's take the compliance track first.

70
00:04:17.840 --> 00:04:22.978
The compliance track is for the people
who read evidence rather than build it:

71
00:04:22.978 --> 00:04:27.152
ISSOs, compliance staff, assessors
and authorizing officials.

72
00:04:27.152 --> 00:04:29.978
It teaches one skill above all, tracing.

73
00:04:29.978 --> 00:04:34.421
Given a control, name the mechanism
that closes it, the book that built it,

74
00:04:34.421 --> 00:04:39.166
the record it emits, and where that
record sits in the authorization package.

75
00:04:39.166 --> 00:04:43.715
Given a record, work back to its control
and say whether it's still current.

76
00:04:43.715 --> 00:04:49.013
A foundations module covers the essentials,
like the Risk Management Framework,

77
00:04:49.013 --> 00:04:54.153
who owes which proof, and why Moderate and
FedRAMP's Class C are two facts, not one.

78
00:04:54.153 --> 00:04:58.840
Then the modules follow the program's evidence
areas, and each teaches a discipline.

79
00:04:58.840 --> 00:05:02.296
For example, a policy
screenshot can't show a sign-in

80
00:05:02.296 --> 00:05:05.752
was phishing-resistant; only
the sign-in record can.

81
00:05:05.752 --> 00:05:09.739
The capstone is a continuous-monitoring
narrative for one system.

82
00:05:09.739 --> 00:05:11.730
Next, the builders.

83
00:05:14.690 --> 00:05:19.324
The implementation track teaches the
same estate from the engineer's chair,

84
00:05:19.324 --> 00:05:24.178
in build modules from DNS and address
management through to day-two operations.

85
00:05:24.178 --> 00:05:26.452
The order is a real dependency chain.

86
00:05:26.452 --> 00:05:30.997
You address before you identify,
identify before you shape transport,

87
00:05:30.997 --> 00:05:35.777
and so on, and day two comes last because
it runs what everything else built.

88
00:05:35.777 --> 00:05:39.190
Every module ends the same
way: done means it works and

89
00:05:39.190 --> 00:05:42.602
its evidence has landed where
an assessor can read it.

90
00:05:42.602 --> 00:05:45.002
And every lab teaches three habits.

91
00:05:45.002 --> 00:05:46.840
Observe before you enforce.

92
00:05:46.840 --> 00:05:51.103
Keep a way back in, with emergency
access accounts created first.

93
00:05:51.103 --> 00:05:53.765
And export the record, not a screenshot.

94
00:05:53.765 --> 00:05:57.615
Certifications are examples,
not proof, and they change.

95
00:05:57.615 --> 00:06:01.813
One major Microsoft security exam
was just retired and replaced,

96
00:06:01.813 --> 00:06:05.777
so the program re-checks its
certification list at every gate.

97
00:06:05.777 --> 00:06:08.290
So how does the program certify people?

98
00:06:12.040 --> 00:06:14.815
Federal guidance asks
for more than completion.

99
00:06:14.815 --> 00:06:17.008
It asks agencies to test knowledge,

100
00:06:17.008 --> 00:06:21.015
ideally with practical assessments
that mimic the actual work.

101
00:06:21.015 --> 00:06:24.047
So the program certifies
only what a person can show,

102
00:06:24.047 --> 00:06:26.515
with an artifact someone else could check.

103
00:06:26.515 --> 00:06:29.865
Every capstone is scored
on one four-level scale.

104
00:06:29.865 --> 00:06:31.577
At the bottom, it's missing.

105
00:06:31.577 --> 00:06:34.612
In the middle, it names the
right books but gets the

106
00:06:34.612 --> 00:06:38.314
controls or evidence wrong, or
it traces the chain correctly.

107
00:06:38.314 --> 00:06:41.869
At the top, it's assessor-ready:
a real, dated result,

108
00:06:41.869 --> 00:06:45.690
with an honest statement of
what isn't evaluated yet.

109
00:06:45.690 --> 00:06:48.857
An after-action report with
no findings scores zero,

110
00:06:48.857 --> 00:06:53.128
and so does a training percentage
with no per-person records behind it.

111
00:06:53.128 --> 00:06:57.297
Two credentials follow, one for
builders and one for assessors,

112
00:06:57.297 --> 00:06:59.141
and both expire after a year.

113
00:06:59.141 --> 00:07:03.228
An industry certification can waive
a module, but never a capstone.

114
00:07:03.228 --> 00:07:06.000
Next, vendor training and labs.

115
00:07:09.750 --> 00:07:11.673
Vendors teach their products,

116
00:07:11.673 --> 00:07:15.287
and this book names them as
examples, never endorsements.

117
00:07:15.287 --> 00:07:19.836
A vendor course earns a place only if it
maps to a book procedure, it's current,

118
00:07:19.836 --> 00:07:22.403
the agency can keep the completion record,

119
00:07:22.403 --> 00:07:25.850
and the agency knows how the
vendor handles learner data.

120
00:07:25.850 --> 00:07:28.724
That record point matters,
because exam records

121
00:07:28.724 --> 00:07:31.813
tied to a work account vanish
when the person leaves.

122
00:07:31.813 --> 00:07:35.400
Lab environments are harder to
get than most programs expect.

123
00:07:35.400 --> 00:07:37.125
The free sandboxes are gone,

124
00:07:37.125 --> 00:07:41.100
and the developer sandbox isn't
offered in any government cloud.

125
00:07:41.100 --> 00:07:45.049
So the program office arranges lab
tenants, and before each gate the

126
00:07:45.049 --> 00:07:49.350
team rehearses once in the agency's
own non-production government tenant.

127
00:07:49.350 --> 00:07:53.670
And every lab follows the same rules,
starting with never production,

128
00:07:53.670 --> 00:07:56.550
synthetic data only, and
tear it down at the end.

129
00:07:56.550 --> 00:07:58.360
Now, everyone else.

130
00:08:02.110 --> 00:08:04.214
Every user will sign in differently.

131
00:08:04.214 --> 00:08:08.978
Federal policy requires phishing-resistant
sign-in for staff, contractors

132
00:08:08.978 --> 00:08:13.669
and partners, and passwords, one-time
codes and push approvals don't qualify.

133
00:08:13.669 --> 00:08:18.116
So users learn a PIV card, Windows
Hello for Business or a passkey,

134
00:08:18.116 --> 00:08:20.718
with at least two methods registered.

135
00:08:20.718 --> 00:08:24.805
The course is a handful of
short modules, covering sign-in,

136
00:08:24.805 --> 00:08:28.543
recovery, devices, where
files now live, and reporting.

137
00:08:28.543 --> 00:08:33.088
The last module matters most: what
the help desk will and won't ask.

138
00:08:33.088 --> 00:08:36.702
The help desk becomes a target
the moment users trust it,

139
00:08:36.702 --> 00:08:40.077
and attackers have posed as
IT staff to exploit that.

140
00:08:40.077 --> 00:08:44.905
So users learn that the real help desk
never asks for a password, a PIN or a code,

141
00:08:44.905 --> 00:08:47.586
never asks you to approve
a prompt for them,

142
00:08:47.586 --> 00:08:51.034
and never sends a pass to an
address you give on the call.

143
00:08:51.034 --> 00:08:53.190
Next, who owns all this.

144
00:08:56.940 --> 00:09:00.390
Ask who owns training and
three offices answer at once.

145
00:09:00.390 --> 00:09:05.115
Between them, nobody owns whether people
can do the new job on the day it changes.

146
00:09:05.115 --> 00:09:07.615
So the program keeps one decider per row.

147
00:09:07.615 --> 00:09:13.240
The sponsor and CIO fund the learning program
and decide each wave's readiness plan.

148
00:09:13.240 --> 00:09:17.089
The CISO owns the training
policy, the completion threshold,

149
00:09:17.089 --> 00:09:21.240
and the exact words users are taught
about sign-in and the help desk.

150
00:09:21.240 --> 00:09:24.301
The human capital office
owns workforce strategy,

151
00:09:24.301 --> 00:09:27.290
position coding, and the
facts behind the rosters.

152
00:09:27.290 --> 00:09:30.927
The training lead builds the
curriculum and reports the numbers,

153
00:09:30.927 --> 00:09:32.678
but never declares a wave ready.

154
00:09:32.678 --> 00:09:34.424
Supervisors own completion,

155
00:09:34.424 --> 00:09:37.990
and the help desk manager decides
whether the desk is ready.

156
00:09:37.990 --> 00:09:40.452
Contractors can build and deliver it all,

157
00:09:40.452 --> 00:09:43.203
but government officials
make the decisions.

158
00:09:43.203 --> 00:09:45.210
Next, the calendar.

159
00:09:48.960 --> 00:09:53.335
The curriculum runs on the roadmap's
calendar, and the planning rule is simple.

160
00:09:53.335 --> 00:09:57.409
No gate is scheduled without its
training dates, and no wave without

161
00:09:57.409 --> 00:10:01.410
its training window, so a wave that
slips moves its training with it.

162
00:10:01.410 --> 00:10:04.960
Each gate carries a training
check beside its technical exit.

163
00:10:04.960 --> 00:10:08.291
Early on, the help desk is
trained on verification

164
00:10:08.291 --> 00:10:11.460
and administrators on
just-in-time activation.

165
00:10:11.460 --> 00:10:13.735
Users are trained before their waves.

166
00:10:13.735 --> 00:10:18.222
And by the last gate, no course still
teaches an Active Directory procedure.

167
00:10:18.222 --> 00:10:22.010
The help desk goes first: no
technician gets a help desk role

168
00:10:22.010 --> 00:10:25.797
until their training and
verification check are on record.

169
00:10:25.797 --> 00:10:30.227
Before each wave there's a countdown,
starting with champions and managers,

170
00:10:30.227 --> 00:10:34.798
then the help desk, then users with
reminders, and finally the readiness check.

171
00:10:34.798 --> 00:10:36.448
The timings are on the slide.

172
00:10:36.448 --> 00:10:38.240
So how do you know it worked?

173
00:10:41.990 --> 00:10:44.727
A completion record says
someone finished a course.

174
00:10:44.727 --> 00:10:48.366
Readiness says they can do the
job on the day the change arrives.

175
00:10:48.366 --> 00:10:52.950
The program measures four levels,
following NIST's learning guide: how people

176
00:10:52.950 --> 00:10:56.174
reacted, what they learned,
how their behavior changed,

177
00:10:56.174 --> 00:10:57.965
and what results the gates saw.

178
00:10:57.965 --> 00:11:02.577
The rule is completion for everyone,
and competence wherever a mistake is

179
00:11:02.577 --> 00:11:07.415
a security event, like help desk
technicians and privileged administrators.

180
00:11:07.415 --> 00:11:11.152
For them, completion without a
passing assessment doesn't count.

181
00:11:11.152 --> 00:11:14.742
For end users, the most direct
measure is sign-in method

182
00:11:14.742 --> 00:11:18.252
registration by wave, and it
costs nothing to collect.

183
00:11:18.252 --> 00:11:21.927
A wave that isn't ready moves
its date, not its threshold.

184
00:11:21.927 --> 00:11:26.455
And the steering committee gets one
page at every meeting, in counts,

185
00:11:26.455 --> 00:11:31.064
not names, with privileged gaps
counted, because each one is a finding.

186
00:11:31.064 --> 00:11:33.290
That brings us to the decisions.

187
00:11:37.040 --> 00:11:38.415
So, four decisions.

188
00:11:38.415 --> 00:11:44.273
Put training in every gate, measured against
a roster from HR and identity records,

189
00:11:44.273 --> 00:11:49.615
not a headcount, and let a gate pass with
a gap only as a named, dated exception.

190
00:11:49.615 --> 00:11:55.303
Train and verify the help desk first,
before any new role and before every wave.

191
00:11:55.303 --> 00:11:57.560
Name the owners and keep them apart:

192
00:11:57.560 --> 00:12:01.064
the human capital office and
the CIO set competencies,

193
00:12:01.064 --> 00:12:06.515
the CISO owns the security words, and the
training lead reports while others decide.

194
00:12:06.515 --> 00:12:09.928
And certify with artifacts,
renewed every year.

195
00:12:09.928 --> 00:12:12.128
The next step needs no procurement.

196
00:12:12.128 --> 00:12:15.911
Build the role roster from HR
records and role assignments,

197
00:12:15.911 --> 00:12:20.929
name a training lead and an owner for each
track, and brief the workforce office,

198
00:12:20.929 --> 00:12:25.716
labor relations and the Section 508
program manager before the first gate.

199
00:12:25.716 --> 00:12:28.478
People, not tools, close the gap.

200
00:12:28.478 --> 00:12:33.339
People: Training and Education has
the detail, every claim footnoted.

201
00:12:33.339 --> 00:12:34.160
Thank you.
